WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:16.806 --> 00:00:20.960
We know that people who
fit in enjoy their work.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.050
They enjoy the experience of
being in an organization.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.510
They're emotionally rewarded by it.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:32.611
And we all aspire, I imagine,
to create organizations where

00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:38.201
members of the organization wake up
in the morning and want to come and

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:43.203
where they feel attached and
indebted to the organization,

00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:46.265
and mostly indebted to their peers.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:54.248
And by creating a healthy cultural
environment, we can facilitate that.

00:00:54.248 --> 00:00:58.565
[MUSIC]

00:00:58.565 --> 00:01:03.534
It's the experience of coming to
an entirely different cultural context,

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:06.960
that makes visible to you
how pervasive culture is.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.500
But also how invisible it is.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.024
So, like an example I always like to
use is when I came to the States for

00:01:13.024 --> 00:01:18.016
the first time and people would say to me,
let's have coffee sometime, and

00:01:18.016 --> 00:01:22.561
I would take that as a literal
invitation to having coffee sometime.

00:01:22.561 --> 00:01:26.369
And I didn't quite understand that in
was fact like a very nice pleasant

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:30.876
way to finish a conversation, but where
the intention is actually, I don't want to

00:01:30.876 --> 00:01:35.090
have coffee with you, but let's just
pretend that we had a nice interaction.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.500
And then so I would initially
respond by saying, yeah, sure,

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.570
let's have coffee sometime, when?

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.000
It took me a while to figure out that
that wasn't the intention of the speaker.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.520
And it's only when you're kind of
transposed to very different cultural

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.780
settings that suddenly your lack of
cultural skill becomes apparent to you.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:02.346
And that moment to me kind of
highlighted how important but

00:02:02.346 --> 00:02:09.188
invisible it is, and
why it's important for us to study it.

00:02:09.188 --> 00:02:13.185
[MUSIC]

00:02:13.185 --> 00:02:18.104
Culture is about the systematic
distribution of beliefs and

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:22.780
cognitive understandings
across a population.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.560
And it's very difficult to
measure people's cognition.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.870
Historically we've been doing this by
asking them questions, through surveys.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.640
Problem is that surveys
are not very good instruments,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.990
because we lie to ourselves,
we lie to others.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:46.130
We sometimes are not very well
attached to our cognition and

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:52.522
especially our un-self-conscious cognition
acts, what makes it un-self-conscious.

00:02:52.522 --> 00:02:56.840
Add to that the fact that it's not
very feasible to ask people questions

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.020
every day, time and again.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.193
It leads to huge attrition and
disengagement.

00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:07.435
[MUSIC]

00:03:07.435 --> 00:03:12.263
The Internet revolution has
made a variety of behaviors

00:03:12.263 --> 00:03:17.130
that up until recently were
not recorded recordable.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.730
And they leave a trace,
from how we communicate online to

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.050
our purchasing behaviors,
everything now leaves a trace.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.740
And marry that with the computational
capabilities that we now have.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.787
Our ability just our ability to compute,
on our smartphones we now

00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:41.664
have computational capabilities that
are akin to what NASA had in 1985.

00:03:41.664 --> 00:03:45.739
And that, with new machine learning tools,

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:49.815
artificial intelligence, deep learning,

00:03:49.815 --> 00:03:54.643
the ability to analyze
unstructured complex data and

00:03:54.643 --> 00:03:59.253
to reduce it to measurable outcomes,
both of these

00:03:59.253 --> 00:04:04.534
together bring us to the golden
age of cultural analysis.

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:09.068
[MUSIC]

00:04:09.068 --> 00:04:13.898
So we were very lucky to have
collaborated with a few organizations

00:04:13.898 --> 00:04:19.074
already that were willing to share
information deriving from the email

00:04:19.074 --> 00:04:24.700
exchange amongst their employees
over a period of multiple years.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:32.179
By now, myself, my colleagues and all the
members of our lab, we've analyzed many,

00:04:32.179 --> 00:04:38.737
many years of data being exchanged by
members of multiple organizations And

00:04:38.737 --> 00:04:43.860
there's a lot of information
culturally that we can derive

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.593
by looking at how people use
language in their emails.

00:04:48.593 --> 00:04:52.413
[MUSIC]

00:04:52.413 --> 00:04:57.427
The reason that language is such
a useful way for us to analyze culture

00:04:57.427 --> 00:05:02.269
is because language is probably
the most important medium through

00:05:02.269 --> 00:05:07.690
which cultural information is
exchanged between individuals.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.150
I'm not interested in whether when you and

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.670
I are talking are we talking
about how are we going to address

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:19.690
this particular customer problem, or
are we going to talk about HR issues, or

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:24.340
are we going to talk about
the Christmas party that's coming up?

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:29.980
What functionally we're exchanging
is immaterial to the analysis.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:34.593
What's interesting is whether in
discussing these topics, we're using

00:05:34.593 --> 00:05:39.439
the same kind of linguistic styles as
a reflection of our cultural alignment.

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:42.922
So whether we're using aggressive and

00:05:42.922 --> 00:05:47.582
brief language or
whether we're very apologetic.

00:05:47.582 --> 00:05:51.250
And you can just think about all your
email exchanges, there's a significant

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:56.850
difference between saying dear so and so,
it was such a pleasure to meet you and

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:00.260
I hope that this is not
an intrusion on your time, etc.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:05.140
But if you don't mind, at your earliest
convenience, can we please meet?

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:10.660
And you can say the exact same thing but
say, hey John, can we meet next week?

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:15.980
Without all of the introductions and
apologies.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.960
Functionally you've exchanged
the exact same information, but

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.240
there's a lot of cultural content embedded
in how you chose to present yourself and

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.930
the problem and the question.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.505
And that's precisely what
our analysis tries to infer.

00:06:30.505 --> 00:06:34.704
[MUSIC]

00:06:34.704 --> 00:06:39.020
Swear words are very, from a symbolic
point of view, they're very aggressive.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.066
Somebody drops an F bomb, it's noticeable.

00:06:42.066 --> 00:06:47.760
It's not a subtle thing that
people do as they speak.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.210
And if you think about the organizations
that you've been in or

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:54.899
the various cultural contexts in which
you've gone through through your life,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:07:00.790
there's a lot of variation in
terms of whether it's allowed or

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.970
really a faux pas to use
swear words in interaction.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:08.970
And you can imagine that there
are certain organisations where not only

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.552
do people swear a lot,

00:07:10.552 --> 00:07:16.566
they use swearing as a means to project to
others their authority and their tenacity.

00:07:16.566 --> 00:07:21.491
And you can imagine, at the other extreme,
that there are organizations where

00:07:21.491 --> 00:07:24.799
the use of swear words is
perceived as an offensive and

00:07:24.799 --> 00:07:30.310
inappropriate way of handling yourself and
would be interpreted as aggression.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.216
So it's the same use of language, but
it's interpreted very differently as

00:07:35.216 --> 00:07:38.387
a function of what's
normatively appropriate or

00:07:38.387 --> 00:07:41.569
inappropriate in these
two different settings.

00:07:41.569 --> 00:07:46.039
[MUSIC]

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:50.755
In the research that I do and where we
analyze how people use language in their

00:07:50.755 --> 00:07:55.915
interactions, their habitual way of
speaking inside the organization, whether

00:07:55.915 --> 00:08:00.852
they swear at the appropriate level is a
very strong signal of the extent to which

00:08:00.852 --> 00:08:05.968
they're aligned or misaligned with
the normative expectations of their peers.

00:08:05.968 --> 00:08:11.439
The mere information about whether or not
an individual is correctly using language

00:08:11.439 --> 00:08:16.755
insofar as how language is being used by
others and reflecting their expectations

00:08:16.755 --> 00:08:21.684
and beliefs about the world is a very
strong indicator of whether that person

00:08:21.684 --> 00:08:26.615
reads the cultural code and whether
conditional having read it correctly,

00:08:26.615 --> 00:08:31.883
whether that person has the motivation
to behave in a culturally compliant way.

00:08:31.883 --> 00:08:36.067
[MUSIC]

00:08:36.067 --> 00:08:40.828
So you can imagine that some people
realize that it's inappropriate to swear,

00:08:40.828 --> 00:08:42.710
but they swear nevertheless.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:43.680
And that decision,

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.510
whether conscious or un-self-conscious,
has a lot of information.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.630
They're either signaling to others that

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:57.770
they don't want to be a part of
this group, or they might feel so

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:02.220
secure in their position in
the group that they believe that

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.020
by behaving in a non-conformist way,
they can signal to others their

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.010
uniqueness without undermining
their position in the organization.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:13.785
So there's a lot of information
in these very subtle and

00:09:13.785 --> 00:09:19.585
automatic decisions that we make and how
we communicate information to one another.

00:09:19.585 --> 00:09:23.818
[MUSIC]

00:09:23.818 --> 00:09:28.740
Discoveries on culture are both
exciting and daunting.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.892
They're exciting because
we're starting to figure out

00:09:32.892 --> 00:09:39.040
the constituent components of this
opaque thing that we call culture.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.670
They're daunting because we discover how
complex it is, how difficult it is to

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:48.420
manage, and how not one size fit all or
one shot exercise it is.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.803
But again, they're exciting
because I think we're at this

00:09:53.803 --> 00:09:59.291
moment where the research can
be translated into insights and

00:09:59.291 --> 00:10:04.779
into methodologies and
into tools that can inform managerial

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:10.477
decisions in a way that would make
management more effective and

00:10:10.477 --> 00:10:14.092
more rewarding to everybody involved.

00:10:14.092 --> 00:10:18.629
[MUSIC]

